  "content": {
    "id": "p64-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Show that $P(x)=x^3+x+3$ does not have an integer zero by testing all the possible integer zeros.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$P(-3)=-27$, $P(-1)=1$, $P(1)=5$, $P(3)=33$. None is zero, so there is no integer zero.",
      "worked": "Any integer zero must be a factor of $3$, so test $-3,-1,1,3$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}P(-3)&=(-3)^3+(-3)+3=-27\\\\P(-1)&=(-1)^3+(-1)+3=1\\\\P(1)&=1^3+1+3=5\\\\P(3)&=3^3+3+3=33\\end{aligned}$$\nNone of the possible integer zeros gives $P(x)=0$. Therefore $P(x)$ has no integer zero.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 42
  },
